description:
Two sites use the .ok().and_then(...).ok() chain to silently drop serde errors during filter parsing — a classic error-masking anti-pattern that the supplement flags under error-handling.

Site 1 — src/memory.rs:378-384 (MemoryBackend::query_objects):

    let fc: Option<jmap_filenode_types::FileNodeFilterCondition> = if let Some(f) = filter {
        serde_json::to_value(f)
            .ok()                                                    // <- drops to_value error
            .and_then(|v| serde_json::from_value(v).ok())            // <- drops from_value error
    } else {
        None
    };
    ...
    let mut ids: Vec<Id> = store.nodes.values()
        .filter(|node| node_matches_filter(node, fc.as_ref()))
        .map(...).collect();

If to_value or from_value errors, fc becomes None. node_matches_filter at memory.rs:721-725 returns 'true' when fc is None (the 'no filter' case). Net effect: the client asked for a filter, the filter could not be parsed, the response is 'all nodes match' — silently treated as an unfiltered query.

Site 2 — src/lib.rs:312-318 (MockBackend::query_objects, in test_support):

    if let Ok(json_val) = serde_json::to_value(filter_ref) {        // <- drops to_value error
        if let Ok(fc) = serde_json::from_value::<
            jmap_filenode_types::FileNodeFilterCondition,
        >(json_val)                                                 // <- drops from_value error
        {
            ...
        }
    }

Same pattern, same semantic: silent drop. In a test backend the impact is lower, but the same code is being copied into a future real backend.

What idiomatic Rust would do — two options:

(a) Propagate the error. The fn already returns Result, so:

    let fc: Option<jmap_filenode_types::FileNodeFilterCondition> = match filter {
        None => None,
        Some(f) => {
            let v = serde_json::to_value(f)
                .map_err(|e| MemoryError(format!("serialize filter: {e}")))?;
            Some(serde_json::from_value(v)
                .map_err(|e| MemoryError(format!("parse filter: {e}")))?)
        }
    };

(b) If best-effort is genuinely intended, document it in the function doc AND make the silent-drop visible: log a warning, or return Err with a 'filter could not be parsed' diagnostic. Currently neither is true.

The .ok() chain reads to a casual reviewer as 'parse if possible, ignore on error'. The supplement specifically calls this out: '\.unwrap_or(default_value) where the failure is genuinely an error (malformed input) is non-idiomatic — you want .parse()? or match to propagate.' The .ok().and_then(.ok()) shape is the same anti-pattern wearing a hat.

Workspace context: the silent-error policy is already documented in workspace AGENTS.md (Filter algebra exclusion section, reason #1): 'Silent-drop is a server-side query-correctness bug.' This finding is consistent with that policy.

Cross-reference: JMAP-510h.9 (MemoryBackend filter only matches parentId and isTopLevel; other conditions are silently passed through) flags the DOWNSTREAM side of the same bug — node_matches_filter's incomplete coverage. The current finding flags the UPSTREAM side — the filter never gets a chance to be applied because the parse silently fails. They are different defects in the same call chain.
